---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CalcMoneyVat from '@/components/tools/finance/CalcMoneyVat.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="gst-calculator">
  <CalcMoneyVat client:load variant="gst" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>The two shortcuts worth memorising</h2>
    <p>
      In Australia, GST is 10%, so the GST arithmetic is easy:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>To add GST, multiply the ex-GST price by 1.1.</li>
      <li>To find the GST in a total, divide by 11.</li>
      <li>To find the ex-GST price, divide the total by 1.1.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      New Zealand's 15% is less tidy. Divide the inclusive total by 1.15 for the ex-GST price, and by
      7.6667 for the GST. The three-sevenths shortcut from the old 12.5% rate has been wrong since
      October 2010.
    </p>

    <h2>Rates in this calculator</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[460px] border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Country</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Rate</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Divide total by</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Tax fraction of total</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="tabular-nums">
          <tr><td>Australia (GST)</td><td style="text-align:right">10%</td><td style="text-align:right">1.10</td><td style="text-align:right">1/11</td></tr>
          <tr><td>New Zealand (GST)</td><td style="text-align:right">15%</td><td style="text-align:right">1.15</td><td style="text-align:right">3/23</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Singapore (GST)</td><td style="text-align:right">9%</td><td style="text-align:right">1.09</td><td style="text-align:right">9/109</td></tr>
          <tr><td>India (GST slabs)</td><td style="text-align:right">5 / 12 / 18 / 28%</td><td style="text-align:right">1.05–1.28</td><td style="text-align:right">varies</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Canada (GST)</td><td style="text-align:right">5%</td><td style="text-align:right">1.05</td><td style="text-align:right">1/21</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Canada (HST, Ontario)</td><td style="text-align:right">13%</td><td style="text-align:right">1.13</td><td style="text-align:right">13/113</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Canada (HST, Atlantic)</td><td style="text-align:right">15%</td><td style="text-align:right">1.15</td><td style="text-align:right">3/23</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Malaysia (SST, services)</td><td style="text-align:right">6%</td><td style="text-align:right">1.06</td><td style="text-align:right">3/53</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text-sm">
      The rates are static data in the page, with the date they were checked. The site does not fetch
      them live. Confirm the current rate with your revenue authority before you file, and type a custom
      rate when you need one.
    </p>

    <h2>India's slab system</h2>
    <p>
      India does not have a single GST rate. The GST Council puts goods and services in slabs, usually
      5%, 12%, 18% and 28%. Some items are zero-rated, and a short list of luxury and demerit goods pays
      a compensation cess on top of 28%. The slab follows your HSN code for goods or SAC code for
      services.
    </p>
    <p>
      The tax also splits by destination. A sale within a state is CGST plus SGST, each half the total
      rate. A sale across state lines is IGST at the full rate. The split changes how you report the
      tax, but the amount and the arithmetic above stay the same.
    </p>

    <h2>Canada: GST, PST and HST</h2>
    <p>
      Three regimes coexist. Alberta and the territories charge only the 5% federal GST. Ontario and
      the Atlantic provinces use a Harmonised Sales Tax that combines federal and provincial into one
      rate of 13% or 15%. British Columbia, Saskatchewan and Manitoba charge 5% GST plus a separate
      provincial sales tax, and Quebec charges GST plus QST.
    </p>
    <p>
      For a separate-PST province, enter the two taxes as two lines in the invoice panel. Do not add the
      rates together: they are administered separately, and in Quebec the QST base rules differ from GST.
    </p>

    <h2>GST or VAT? Structurally the same tax</h2>
    <p>
      Both are broad-based consumption taxes collected at each stage of the supply chain. Businesses
      claim credit for tax already paid on their inputs, so only the final consumer pays. Australia, New
      Zealand, India, Canada and Singapore say GST. The UK, the EU and South Africa say VAT. The name is
      historical, and the arithmetic on this page is the same for both.
    </p>
    <p>
      A US sales tax is different. It is charged only at the final retail sale and has no input credit.
    </p>

    <h2>Invoices and rounding</h2>
    <p>
      The multi-line panel takes a full invoice with a different rate per line, for Indian slabs and for
      mixed supplies. Each line is rounded to the minor unit on its own, as accounting software does, and
      the totals are the sum of the rounded lines. Some authorities require you to round the total
      instead. The difference is at most one cent per line, but use one method consistently.
    </p>

    <h2>Registration thresholds</h2>
    <p>
      Australia requires GST registration above A$75,000 of turnover (A$150,000 for non-profits),
      New Zealand above NZ$60,000, and Singapore above S$1 million. Below the threshold, registration
      is usually optional. It can still help if you sell mainly to other registered businesses: they
      reclaim what you charge, and you reclaim the GST on your own costs.
    </p>

    <h2>Everything runs on your device</h2>
    <p>
      Turnover, invoice values and client work stay in this browser tab, and the CSV, Excel and PDF
      exports are made locally. The GST calculator also works offline.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
